From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Atharva Tyagi <tyagiatharva11@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 11:18:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] In-process LPDM hand-off: the LES stops writing its window to
 a filesystem

A footprint case saved every output-cadence snapshot of the fields the backward LPDM
reads and then read them straight back. Nothing else consumed those files. At 122^3
with a 5 s cadence a 2700 s window is 541 dumps of 18.4 MB = 9.4 GB, a two-window case
is ~20 GB, and a ~2900-pair corpus writes and re-reads on the order of 14 TB. On a
rented GPU whose scratch performance and quota cannot be measured in advance that was
the largest unquantified risk in the corpus plan, and it bought nothing: the encode and
the decode are pure overhead between two parts of the same computation.

SRC/IO/io_lpdmonline.c stages each snapshot from ioBuffField at exactly the point the
netCDF writer would consume it -- interior, halos trimmed, transposed to kji,
rho-divided -- into a tmpfs directory that the consumer (lpdm/ringsrc.py) reads and
pushes into the VRAM ring. Behind lpdmOnlineSelector, PARAM_OPTIONAL and defaulting to
0, so every tutorial case and every existing .in runs bit-identically.

Selector 2 stages AND writes. That is not a convenience: it is the only configuration
in which the two paths are comparable, because comparing across two LES runs would
difference two turbulence realisations, which on this project differ by 44% in the
footprint integral. MEASURED that way, over 23 snapshots: u, v, w, theta, TKE_0,
fricVel, z0m and invOblen agree EXACTLY, max |diff| 0.000e+00, and window_stats and
FieldSet through both sources are bit-identical.

A full ioLPDMfullFrq dump still writes everything even under selector 1. It is the
only artifact a staged run leaves behind, and the htFlux/z0m read-back assertions,
k0/k1 and turb_alive all score it -- suppressing its fields would delete the evidence
that the run was the run that was asked for.

WHY tmpfs FILES AND NOT CUDA IPC. lpdmDevicePushSnapshotHost takes HOST pointers by
design: the wrap-pad, the fp16 cast and the eps/dsig2dz derivation are the device
kernels already validated against the CPU path. A host hop is inherent to that API and
costs ~4 ms of PCIe per 5 s of model time against ~2.5 s of compute for the same
interval. IPC would buy that 4 ms and cost a new packing kernel on the critical path
of a validated integrator. The ring is still in VRAM; only the route in is host memory.

WHY WHOLE FILES AND NOT A LOCK-FREE RING. Correctness under a crash. A producer and a
consumer sharing a ring must agree on recovery when either dies mid-window; a
directory of whole files with a separate completion marker cannot be observed
half-written, needs no atomics, and leaves the queue state visible to ls. Backpressure
counts snap.*.ok in the directory rather than trusting a flag the consumer must clear,
so a dead consumer stalls the LES loudly instead of filling tmpfs and dying on ENOSPC
several hundred dumps later.

A pause is not a restart -- no exit, no re-read, no IO-registered field overwritten --
so the restart-read trap does not reach it. It exists because the sigma_w floor is
built from WHOLE-WINDOW statistics, so the ensemble cannot be released until the last
snapshot of the window is in the ring. That is also why the ring holds a full window
and not t_back: a shorter ring forces integration at each release time, which forces
the floor onto partial-window statistics -- an estimator change wearing a plumbing
change's clothes.

Two defects found by running it, both fixed here. lpdmOnlinePause1/2 used 0 as the
"disabled" sentinel and 0 is a real step, so the first run paused at the very first
output and waited for a resume nobody would write. And the queue-depth wait originally
polled for a flag file that nothing created.
